THINK = {
1: r"Always ask what a number means before using it. A price tells you little, but a return says how much you gained for each unit invested, and volatility says how bumpy the ride was. Being clear about these two ideas prevents the commonest beginner mistakes.",
2: r"Most statistical tools assume that the world does not change its rules while you watch. Stationarity is the check that this assumption is reasonable. If a series fails it, transform it (usually by taking changes) before testing anything.",
3: r"A market is a queue, and the price you actually get depends on how much you buy and who is in line. Reading the order book is like reading the crowd at a shop counter. Knowing how matching works turns ``slippage'' from a mystery into arithmetic.",
4: r"A perpetual future is a bet that never expires, kept honest by a small payment between buyers and sellers. Once you see funding as a price for holding a position, the contract's behaviour follows. Always work out what you pay or earn per day.",
5: r"A prediction market turns beliefs into prices. To tell whether forecasters are any good, score them with rules that reward honesty, and check calibration: of the events called $70\%$, did about $70\%$ happen?",
6: r"An AMM replaces a crowd of traders with a rule. The rule makes the pool always willing to trade, but at a price that moves as you trade. Impermanent loss is the cost of that arrangement: you would have done better just holding.",
7: r"Data work is unglamorous and it decides everything. Treat every cleaning step as a decision you can explain, and keep the raw data untouched. Time is the trap: clocks, time zones and calendars all hide ways to leak the future into the past.",
8: r"A backtest is a story about the past, and it is only as trustworthy as the code that runs it. Test the harness on cases where you know the answer. The most dangerous bug is the one that lets the strategy peek at tomorrow.",
9: r"If you try a hundred ideas, a few will look brilliant by chance. Honest research is the habit of counting every attempt and asking how impressive the best one would be if all of them were random. Be most suspicious when the result is best.",
10: r"A good idea can be ruined by poor execution. Large orders move the market against you, so cost grows with size. Ask of every strategy, ``at what size does this stop working?''",
11: r"Time series must be tested in time order: train on the past, test on what comes next. Walk-forward validation repeats this over many windows so that one lucky split cannot fool you. Leave gaps so that overlapping data cannot leak.",
12: r"A performance number is an estimate with error bars. Report the interval along with the value, and prefer several simple metrics to one clever one. A suspiciously high ratio is more often a bug than a discovery.",
13: r"Kelly sizing answers ``how much should I risk?'' by maximising long-run growth, and it punishes overconfidence. Since your edge is only an estimate, bet a fraction of what the formula says. Survival comes before growth.",
14: r"Risk is about the bad days you have not seen yet. Measures like VaR and expected shortfall put a number on them, and stress tests ask what happens in cases history has not offered. No single number is enough, so look at several.",
15: r"Research is only useful if someone else can read it, check it and rerun it. Write the claim first, show the evidence next, and state what would change your mind. Try to break your own result before anyone else does.",
16: r"Assets that move together offer less protection than their count suggests. Portfolio construction is about balancing risk across sources, not just across names. Ask where the common risk is hiding.",
17: r"This chapter is a rehearsal of the whole method: one question, data you can trust, a measurement, an honest test, a sized position and a report. The point is the discipline, not the strategy; ask what result would make you stop.",
18: r"A second pass teaches you to hold a result lightly. Write down in advance what would kill the idea, test against that, and allow the answer to be ``conditional.'' Saying ``not proven'' is a legitimate and useful research result.",
}
